# Trivy ignore list for the runtime container image.
#
# Each entry below is a CVE in a package that `pip` vendors internally
# (pip/_vendor/, see vendor.txt) for its OWN build-time operation — not a
# package our application imports or that pip exposes as an installable
# top-level dependency. Even the latest available pip release vendors these
# exact vulnerable sub-versions, so bumping pip/setuptools in the Dockerfile
# cannot change them. The application never imports or shells out to pip at
# runtime (verified: no `import pip`/`import msgpack`/`import setuptools` and
# no `pip` subprocess calls anywhere under app/), so these vendored code
# paths are unreachable in this deployment.
#
# Re-evaluate when the base image's bundled pip drops/updates these vendored
# versions, or when the Dockerfile moves to a multi-stage build that excludes
# pip from the final image (tracked for the Docker/CI supply-chain hardening
# pass — see SonarCloud docker:S8541/S8544).

# msgpack 1.1.2 vendored in pip/_vendor — out-of-bounds read on Unpacker
# reuse after error. Only reachable via pip's own msgpack usage during
# `pip install`, which never runs in the built image at runtime.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# setuptools 70.3.0 vendored in pip/_vendor — path traversal in the legacy
# PackageIndex (easy_install-style) remote package fetcher. That code path
# is pip build tooling, not used by the application.
CVE-2025-47273
